invariant: INV-26
file: aes_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh aes-equiv
reason: the host object's arm64 key expansion reads word 3 of a round key
  unrotated into word 0 of the temporary, where FIPS 197 takes RotWord, so
  it computes a different key schedule from AES=soft for both key sizes and
  a different block from it. CBMC cannot read an intrinsic, so no proof
  covers this file and bin/aes_equiv_test is the whole of what holds it:
  it runs both implementations over the same keys and blocks and compares
  the round keys and the cipher output byte for byte. The edit lands in a
  table only an arm64 compiler reads, so the script builds that binary for
  arm64 and for x86-64 and runs each under qemu. Without the equivalence
  test this divergence reaches a build that reads every QUIC Initial
  packet wrong and no gate says so.

--- old
static const uint8_t ROT_LAST_WORD_LANES[AES_BLOCK] = {13, 14, 15, 12, 13, 14, 15, 12,
--- new
static const uint8_t ROT_LAST_WORD_LANES[AES_BLOCK] = {12, 13, 14, 15, 13, 14, 15, 12,
